<h1>Introduction to ASP.NET Core MVC</h1>

<p>
    MVC stands for:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Model</li>
    <li>View</li>
    <li>Controller</li>
</ul>

<p>
    MVC is a design pattern used to separate application logic into different components.
</p>

<p>
    Instead of writing everything in one place:
</p>

<ul>
    <li>Model → Handles Data</li>
    <li>View → Handles UI</li>
    <li>Controller → Handles Logic</li>
</ul>

<p>
    MVC helps in creating clean, maintainable, and scalable applications.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>What is MVC?</h2>

<p>
    MVC is a design pattern that separates an application into:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Model</strong> → Data Layer</li>
    <li><strong>View</strong> → UI Layer</li>
    <li><strong>Controller</strong> → Logic Layer</li>
</ul>

<p>
    A request goes through routing to a controller,
    the controller processes data using models,
    and finally returns a view as response.
</p>

<hr />

<h2>Components of MVC</h2>

<hr />

<h3>1. Model (Data Layer)</h3>

<p>
    Model represents application data and business logic.
</p>

<p>
    Models are usually C# classes.
</p>

<h4>Example</h4>

<pre>
<code>
public class Student
{
    public int Id { get; set; }

    public string Name { get; set; }
}
</code>
</pre>

<h4>Responsibilities</h4>

<ul>
    <li>Data handling</li>
    <li>Validation rules</li>
    <li>Business data representation</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h3>2. View (UI Layer)</h3>

<p>
    View is the user interface of the application.
</p>

<p>
    It displays data to the user using HTML and Razor syntax.
</p>

<h4>Example</h4>

<pre>
<code>
<h1>@Model.Name</h1>
</code>
</pre>

<h4>Responsibilities</h4>

<ul>
    <li>UI rendering</li>
    <li>Displaying data</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h3>3. Controller (Logic Layer)</h3>

<p>
    Controller handles incoming HTTP requests.
</p>

<p>
    It connects the Model and View together.
</p>

<h4>Example</h4>

<pre>
<code>
public class StudentController : Controller
{
    public IActionResult Index()
    {
        var student = new Student
        {
            Name = "John"
        };

        return View(student);
    }
}
</code>
</pre>

<h4>Responsibilities</h4>

<ul>
    <li>Request handling</li>
    <li>Business logic execution</li>
    <li>Returning response</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>How MVC Works (Request Flow)</h2>

<pre>
<code>
User enters URL
       ↓
Request goes to Routing System
       ↓
Routing finds Controller + Action
       ↓
Controller executes logic
       ↓
Data comes from Model
       ↓
Controller sends data to View
       ↓
View renders HTML
       ↓
Response goes back to User
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Request Lifecycle in MVC</h2>

<ul>
    <li>Request hits the server</li>
    <li>Middleware pipeline executes</li>
    <li>Routing identifies endpoint</li>
    <li>Controller action executes</li>
    <li>Response is generated</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Create Your First MVC Project</h2>

<pre>
<code>
dotnet new mvc -n MyFirstMvcApp

cd MyFirstMvcApp

dotnet run
</code>
</pre>

<hr />

<h2>Understand MVC Folder Structure</h2>

<p>
    When an MVC project is created, the following folders are generated:
</p>

<ul>
    <li><strong>Controllers/</strong> → Contains all controllers</li>
    <li><strong>Models/</strong> → Contains model classes</li>
    <li><strong>Views/</strong> → Contains Razor UI files</li>
    <li><strong>wwwroot/</strong> → Contains static files like CSS and JavaScript</li>
    <li><strong>Program.cs</strong> → Application startup configuration</li>
</ul>

<hr />

<h2>Important Interview Point</h2>

<p>
    MVC is a design pattern in ASP.NET Core that separates an application into
    Model, View, and Controller to achieve separation of concerns and better maintainability.
</p>